        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-7037104"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://nebius.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A8b69336d-51ba-4e44-920f-ddf22872c09f" class="user-hover" rel="712020:8b69336d-51ba-4e44-920f-ddf22872c09f" data-account-id="712020:8b69336d-51ba-4e44-920f-ddf22872c09f" accountid="712020:8b69336d-51ba-4e44-920f-ddf22872c09f" rel="noreferrer">Andrei Rykov</a> </p>

<blockquote><p>“To avoid confusion … these Grafana links are loaded by tag”. </p></blockquote>

<p>Now I got it) You avoided one confusion but replaced it with another, as it looks now like those tag-based links are not only Grafana-related. I would suggest rephrasing ‘If tag-based links are configured…’ to ‘If <b>Grafana</b> tag-based links are configured…’ to avoid both confusions.</p>

<p>The question about alternate sources is also pulled off in this regard.</p>

<blockquote><p>We need to support links to non-Grafana resources too, for example, for diagnostics or documentation.</p></blockquote>

<p>Other sources without Grafana specifics, just static links – yes, it must remain, yet we decided not to improve it anyhow as part of this ticket, just leave 3 existing options.</p>

<blockquote><p> I think the Grafana block should handle other sources as well.</p></blockquote>

<p>May I ask you to elaborate on that? </p>

<blockquote><p>One of the steps in the scenario… <tt>a new tab opens in Grafana with auto-filled parameters (ds, workspace)</tt></p></blockquote>

<p>Yes I saw it, and this last step is ok. But I’m suggesting to add information about forging links to the step when they are loaded/displayed, to reveal the technological magic behind the last step.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-7039148"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Комментарий по  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:8b69336d-51ba-4e44-920f-ddf22872c09f"
                id="word_commented_712020:8b69336d-51ba-4e44-920f-ddf22872c09f"
                href="https://nebius.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A8b69336d-51ba-4e44-920f-ddf22872c09f">
                    Andrei Rykov
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">11/ноя/25</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-7039148"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://nebius.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A9b6f6940-7c5c-486d-b283-abfb0ef8b2e7" class="user-hover" rel="712020:9b6f6940-7c5c-486d-b283-abfb0ef8b2e7" data-account-id="712020:9b6f6940-7c5c-486d-b283-abfb0ef8b2e7" accountid="712020:9b6f6940-7c5c-486d-b283-abfb0ef8b2e7" rel="noreferrer">Alexander Smirnov</a> </p>

<blockquote><p>Re performing a request to Grafana before rendering the page, may I ask you to come up with other options? We can then compare them, and decide rationally why to choose which. Discussing only one option is not practical - there can be indefinite number of reasons both ‘for’ and ‘against’.</p></blockquote>

<p>I selected 4 possible approaches. You’ll see that some of these options are similar in terms of when data is loaded or how the links are shown. Below are implementation options with steps, pros and cons.</p>

<p><b>Option 1. UI preloads Grafana links and shows all links as a vertical list of links.</b></p>

<h3><a name="Steps%3A"></a>Steps:</h3>
<ol>
	<li>User goes to the cluster page.</li>
	<li>UI performs the initial render and shows a loading spinner.</li>
	<li>UI sends the Grafana request asynchronously on page load.</li>
	<li>UI waits for Grafana response or timeout (1 sec).</li>
	<li>UI renders the page and displays available links.<br/>
<b>Successful Grafana load:</b></li>
</ol>
<ul>
	<li>UI shows vertical list of static links and Grafana tag-based links with filled parameters (ds, workspace, etc).<br/>
<b>Failed Grafana load:</b></li>
	<li>UI shows only static links.</li>
	<li>UI shows a warning such as “Failed to load Grafana dashboards”.
<h3><a name="Pros"></a>Pros</h3></li>
	<li>No UI layout shifts.</li>
	<li>No clicks or other user actions are required to see the links.
<h3><a name="Cons"></a>Cons</h3></li>
	<li>The list of links may take up space.</li>
	<li>Unused request if user never opens <b>info <img class="emoticon" src="/images/icons/emoticons/information.png" height="16" width="16" align="absmiddle" alt="" border="0"/></b> icon in database links scenario.</li>
</ul>


<p><b>Option 2. UI preloads Grafana links and shows a Grafana badge</b></p>

<h3><a name="Steps%3A"></a>Steps:</h3>
<ol>
	<li>User goes to the cluster page.</li>
	<li>UI performs the initial render and shows a page-level loading spinner.</li>
	<li>UI sends Grafana request on page load.</li>
	<li>UI waits for Grafana response or timeout to complete.</li>
	<li>UI shows the page. It shows legacy static links + separate badge for Grafana dashboards. Optional: the button may contain an icon, the name Grafana, and the number of known dashboards.<br/>
<span class="image-wrap" style=""><img src="/rest/api/3/attachment/content/851352" alt="&quot;ChatGPT Image Nov 11" width="149" style="border: 0px solid black" /></span></li>
	<li>User hovers Grafana badge.</li>
	<li>UI shows pop-up.<br/>
<b>Successful Grafana load:</b></li>
</ol>
<ul>
	<li>Pop-up shows vertical list of Grafana static links + Grafana tag-based links with filled parameters (ds, workspace, etc).<br/>
<b>Failed Grafana load:</b></li>
	<li>Pop-up shows vertical list of Grafana static links.</li>
	<li>Pop-up shows a warning such as “Failed to load Grafana dashboards”.
<h3><a name="Pros"></a>Pros</h3></li>
	<li>No UI layout shifts.</li>
	<li>All Grafana links in one space.
<h3><a name="Cons"></a>Cons</h3></li>
	<li>Higher frontend complexity.</li>
	<li>Unused request if user never opens Grafana badge.</li>
</ul>


<p><b>Option 3. UI shows a Grafana badge and load Grafana links on hover.</b></p>

<h3><a name="Steps%3A"></a>Steps:</h3>
<ol>
	<li>User goes to the cluster page.</li>
	<li>UI performs the initial render and shows a page-level loading spinner.</li>
	<li>UI shows the page. It shows a list of legacy static links + separate badge for Grafana dashboards. Optional: the button may contain an icon, the name Grafana, and the number of known dashboards.<br/>
<span class="image-wrap" style=""><img src="/rest/api/3/attachment/content/851353" alt="&quot;ChatGPT Image Nov 11" width="149" style="border: 0px solid black" /></span></li>
	<li>User hovers Grafana button.</li>
	<li>UI shows pop-up with static links immediately. UI shows a skeleton placeholder in place of dashboard links.</li>
	<li>UI sends Grafana request for cluster Grafana tab-based dashboards.</li>
	<li>UI waits for Grafana response.</li>
	<li>When the response arrives, UI replaces the skeleton with the actual dashboard links and filled parameters (ds, workspace, etc).<br/>
<b>Successful Grafana load:</b></li>
</ol>
<ul>
	<li>UI shows static links + Grafana tag-based links with filled parameters (ds, workspace, etc).<br/>
<b>Failed Grafana load:</b></li>
	<li>UI keeps static links.</li>
	<li>UI replaces the skeleton with a error message, for example: “Failed to load Grafana dashboards.”
<h3><a name="Pros"></a>Pros</h3></li>
	<li>Avoid requests if user never interacts with Grafana badge.</li>
	<li>All Grafana links in one space.
<h3><a name="Cons"></a>Cons</h3></li>
	<li>Higher frontend complexity.</li>
	<li>Possible UI layout shifts. Requires UI work to avoid shifts.</li>
</ul>


<p><b>Option 4. Meta resolves dashboards</b></p>

<h3><a name="Steps"></a>Steps</h3>
<ol>
	<li>User goes to the cluster page.</li>
	<li>UI sends cluster info request to Meta.</li>
	<li>Meta fetches dashboards from Grafana. In case of errors, the <tt>grafana</tt> field includes the Grafana response code and text.</li>
	<li>UI receives pre-built link list from Meta.</li>
	<li>UI renders everything without external fetches.<br/>
<b>Successful Grafana load:</b></li>
</ol>
<ul>
	<li>UI shows static links + Grafana tag-based links with filled parameters (ds, workspace, etc).<br/>
<b>Failed Grafana load:</b></li>
	<li>UI shows static links.</li>
	<li>UI shows a warning such as “Failed to load Grafana dashboards.”
<h3><a name="Pros"></a>Pros</h3></li>
	<li>No UI layout shifts.</li>
	<li>Frontend changes are minimal.
<h3><a name="Cons"></a>Cons</h3></li>
	<li>Meta needs access to grafana</li>
	<li>Meta response depends on Grafana access.</li>
</ul>


<p>For myself, I decided that options 1 and 2 are the simplest to implement and more predictable compared to options 3 and 4. I would suggest choosing one of them.</p>
        </td></tr>
                <tr id="comment-header-7039189"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
